Step-by-Step Restart Process for VS Code and PostgreSQL

1. Open VS Code
   - Open your project folder: /Users/admin/Public/Projects/ReactProjectOne/KPL
   - Open the integrated terminal.

2. Change to the backend folder
   cd /Users/admin/Public/Projects/ReactProjectOne/KPL/backend

3. Install dependencies if needed
   npm install

4. Start the backend server in the background
   nohup node server.js > backend.log 2>&1 &

5. Verify backend is running
   lsof -iTCP:3001 -sTCP:LISTEN -n -P
   tail -n 20 backend.log
   curl -sS http://localhost:3001/api/test-db

6. Confirm your database settings in backend/.env
   cat backend/.env
   - DB_NAME=kansarapremierleague_db
   - DB_USER=admin
   - DB_PASSWORD=Kpl@2022
   - DB_HOST=localhost
   - DB_PORT=5432

7. Run a one-shot PostgreSQL query to list recent users
   PGPASSWORD='Kpl@2022' /usr/local/opt/postgresql@16/bin/psql \
     -h localhost -p 5432 -U admin -d kansarapremierleague_db \
     -c "SELECT user_id, full_name, email, created_at FROM users ORDER BY user_id DESC LIMIT 20;"

8. Search for a specific registered email
   PGPASSWORD='Kpl@2022' /usr/local/opt/postgresql@16/bin/psql \
     -h localhost -p 5432 -U admin -d kansarapremierleague_db \
     -c "SELECT user_id, full_name, email, created_at FROM users WHERE email = 'kansara.hiren@outlook.com';"

9. Use interactive psql if needed
   PGPASSWORD='Kpl@2022' /usr/local/opt/postgresql@16/bin/psql \
     -h localhost -p 5432 -U admin -d kansarapremierleague_db

   Then inside psql:
     \d users
     SELECT user_id, full_name, email, created_at FROM users ORDER BY user_id DESC LIMIT 20;
     SELECT user_id, full_name, email, created_at FROM users WHERE email = 'kansara.hiren@outlook.com';
     \q

10. Restart backend if it stops
    cd /Users/admin/Public/Projects/ReactProjectOne/KPL/backend
    pkill -f "node server.js" || true
    nohup node server.js > backend.log 2>&1 &
